vignettes/formula-grammar.Rmd:75:| `y ~ x1 + (1 | id) + (0 + x1 | id)`, `sigma ~ x2`, `family = Gamma(link = "log")` | Implemented first slice | Ordinary Gamma `mu` random intercepts and independent numeric slopes enter the log-mean predictor. A single unlabelled `relmat(1 | id, K = K)` or `relmat(1 + x | id, K = K)` structured intercept or one-slope on `mu` is implemented at recovery grade. Point-estimate recovery has also been assessed for an unlabelled `phylo(1 | id, tree = tree)` intercept. Separate q1 direct-SD point-recovery tests cover `phylo()`, `relmat()`, `animal()` and coordinate-spatial intercepts. A focused native Gamma animal q2 route also fits separate coefficient-selected SD formulas for an intercept and one independent numeric slope using pedigree input; it is not a correlated block. Correlated or labelled slopes, new-route slopes, other structured routes, known covariance, and bivariate or mixed Gamma models remain planned. |
vignettes/formula-grammar.Rmd:81:| `y01 ~ x1 + x2 + (1 | id) + (0 + x1 | id)`, `family = stats::binomial(link = "logit")` | Implemented first slice | Plain Bernoulli response model for explicit 0/1 event data. `mu` is the event probability and there is no public `sigma`; ordinary `mu` random intercepts and independent numeric slopes are fitted. Logit, probit, and complementary log-log links are available. Independent-slope intervals have been assessed only for the designs and limitations in [the reporting guide](capability-and-limits.html), while the exact unlabelled `(1 + x1 | id)` correlated block has point-fit recovery evidence. A native relatedness-matrix intercept with a predictor-dependent SD also has a focused recovery and output test. A separate matching Binomial `relmat()` SD route has one same-data point-parity check in the paired Julia development candidate; this does not establish parity for every native Bernoulli provider route. The Julia bridge additionally admits probit and complementary log-log for one ordinary mean random intercept, one independent numeric mean slope, or their independent intercept-plus-slope pair with separate coefficient-selected `sd()` formulae; one same-data point-recovery fit per link checks the pair. One ordinary correlated q = 3 block now has focused probit, logit, and complementary log-log bridge fits with coefficient-specific SDs for the intercept and two numeric slopes; same-data TMB/Julia coefficient and correlation outputs, group-SD predictions, and marginal simulation pass. The final 80-group probit and 160-group × 32-observation logit/cloglog runs completed without warnings; these point/output checks do not establish interval calibration. The correlated q = 2 probit and cloglog routes and q = 3 probit, logit, and cloglog routes have focused live bridge checks. Lower-information q = 3 logit and cloglog fixtures remain unstable. Factor responses, proportions with trial weights, labelled correlated slopes, other structured-effect combinations, and bivariate responses remain unavailable. |
vignettes/formula-grammar.Rmd:111:| `sd(id) ~ x_group` | Scoped implementation | Models the SD of a location random effect; predictors must be constant within group. Selected native TMB families support ordinary intercept SDs, while Poisson, Gamma, NB2, Beta, Bernoulli, Tweedie, cumulative-logit, and beta-binomial also have focused independent-slope routes. Lognormal additionally admits coefficient-specific SD predictors for one complete-data unlabelled correlated `(1 + x | id)` block, with constant within-block correlation. NB2 additionally supports one complete-data q1 phylogenetic location intercept with `sd(group, level = "phylogenetic") ~ predictors`, fixed `sigma`, and no other random effects. The beta-binomial family also has complete-data `sigma` random-intercept and independent numeric-slope routes with fixed `mu` and group-constant SD predictors. The slope uses `sd(id, dpar = "sigma", coef = "x") ~ w`; one live same-data Julia bridge fit checks this candidate route's point estimates and group-SD predictions. Interval calibration and broader parity remain unverified. One non-inflated Poisson `relmat()` q=1 intercept accepts `sd(id, level = "relmat") ~ z`, with no ordinary `sd()` or zero-inflation formula in that combination. For a Tweedie, cumulative-logit, or beta-binomial slope use `sd(id, coef = "x") ~ w` with `(0 + x | id)`. The cumulative-logit bridge now has separate same-data TMB/Julia fixtures for one ordinary intercept and one numeric slope, checking point recovery and group-level SD prediction; these complete-response checks do not validate interval coverage. Beta and Bernoulli routes have narrower boundaries described below. In the matched Julia development checkout, Binomial also has one `relmat()` q1 intercept with its matching direct-SD formula; that point-parity check is not interval calibration. The remaining family/effect combinations are open. |
vignettes/formula-grammar.Rmd:128:| `y ~ x + phylo(1 | species, tree = tree)`, `sigma ~ z`, `family = beta_family()` | Implemented exact q1 recovery slice | One unlabelled intercept-only phylogenetic location effect in Beta `mu`; family `sigma` remains fixed-effect-only and controls `phi = sigma^(-2)`. Point recovery passed only in the exact tested `g = 1024, m = 4` regime. This is not `g >= 1024`; slopes, labels/q2+, phylogeny in family `sigma`, direct latent-`sd()` regression, REML, intervals, and coverage remain deferred. |
