docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:217:| `sd(id) ~ x_group` | Implemented in scoped routes | Gaussian ordinary `mu` random intercepts and one independent numeric slope selected with `sd(id, coef = "x") ~ w` in native TMB; the paired Julia development bridge has one source-bound q1 slope parity fixture. Native ordinary Bernoulli/binomial `mu` intercepts; native Poisson ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope; complete-data native zero-inflated Poisson `mu` intercepts and one selected independent numeric slope with fixed-effect `zi`; native Gamma ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope; and native complete-data NB2 and zero-truncated NB2 ordinary `mu` intercepts plus Tweedie ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope; native complete-data Beta ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope, with the shared Beta slope slice checked at fixed `sigma ~ 1`; and native complete-data beta-binomial ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope, cumulative-logit ordinary `mu` intercepts and one independent numeric slope, zero-one beta, native lognormal, Student-t, and skew-normal ordinary `mu` intercepts, with independent numeric slopes for native lognormal and skew-normal. Native TMB and Julia also support complete-data zero-one beta `sd(group) ~ predictors` for one ordinary `mu` intercept or one selected independent numeric slope with fixed-intercept `sigma`, `zoi`, and `coi`; structured effects remain unsupported, and interval calibration is open. Julia Bernoulli/binomial SD regression exists only in the DRM.jl development candidate and was tested with explicitly matched local drmTMB and DRM.jl source checkouts; current shared/released DRM.jl does not include these routes. Candidate checks cover separate coefficient-selected SD formulas for independent scalar Bernoulli terms sharing a group, one ordinary correlated q2 intercept-slope block with shared constant correlation, and one relmat q1 intercept with a matching `sd(id, level = "relmat") ~ z` formula. The relmat route has one same-data point-parity fit against native TMB; labelled K/A matrices are aligned by group first appearance, while unlabelled matrices are positional; it does not establish interval calibration. An omitted member in the ordinary correlated block keeps a constant SD. Live R-to-Julia bridge checks in matched candidate checkouts cover fitting, recovery, extraction, prediction, save/reload, profile routing, and three bootstrap refits. A fit emitted a near-singular-Hessian warning; these checks do not establish same-data q2 parity or interval calibration. Other Julia SD routes mentioned in earlier development records likewise require matching candidate source and are not claims about the current shared/released DRM.jl. The candidate Julia Poisson slope route has recovery, prediction, an independent quadrature check, and one same-data comparison with native TMB; candidate Gamma recovery tests also pass. These narrow routes lack interval calibration. The zero-inflated Poisson route keeps `zi` fixed-effect only. Cumulative-logit direct-SD regression requires complete responses and admits one ordinary `mu` intercept or one independent numeric slope selected with `coef`; zero-truncated NB2 requires complete responses and one ordinary `mu` intercept in its direct-SD slice; zero-inflated Poisson requires complete responses and admits one `mu` intercept or one selected independent numeric slope in its direct-SD slice. Hurdle NB2 requires complete responses, fixed-effect `sigma` and `hu`, and one ordinary `mu` intercept or selected independent numeric slope for the paired TMB/Julia direct-SD slice. Zero-one beta requires complete responses and supports one ordinary `mu` intercept or one selected independent numeric `mu` slope in native TMB and Julia. These are separate family/backend slices, not support for every family or random-effect type. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:262:| labelled `relmat(0 + x | p | id, K = K)` or `relmat(0 + x | p | id, Q = Q)` in bivariate `mu1` and `mu2` | Implemented first q=2 slope-only slice; interval and coverage `inference_ready` for the row-level SD targets | Matching labelled lower-level relatedness slope-only terms estimate two location-slope SDs and one `cor(mu1:x,mu2:x | p | id)` correlation from the same known latent matrix. The default `confint()` small-sample correction covers this exact location-axis q2 slope SD row; `supported` is withheld because right-tail miss asymmetry and g-dependence remain measured defects. This is separate from the exact all-four shared-label `relmat(1 + x | p | id, K/Q = ...)` cell; partial or location-only intercept-plus-slope labelled structured covariance remains planned. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:272:| `phylo(0 + x | p | species, tree = tree)` in bivariate `mu1` and `mu2` | Implemented first q=2 slope-only slice; interval and coverage `inference_ready` for the row-level SD targets | Matching labelled phylogenetic slope-only terms estimate two location-slope SDs and one `cor(mu1:x,mu2:x | p | species)` correlation. The default `confint()` small-sample correction covers this exact location-axis q2 slope SD row; `supported` is withheld because right-tail miss asymmetry and g-dependence remain measured defects. The separate shared-label all-four `phylo(1 + x | p | species, tree = tree)` cell has native point-fit/extractor evidence only; bridge parity, broader q8 variants, REML, AI-REML, and public support remain planned. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:1197:| `mu`, `mu1`, `mu2` | Yes for univariate Gaussian `mu`; random intercepts, independent numeric random slopes, and labelled or unlabelled ordinary correlated intercept-slope blocks are implemented. For non-zero-inflated Poisson and NB2 models, ordinary unlabelled `mu` random intercepts and independent numeric slopes such as `(1 | id) + (0 + x | id)` are implemented on the log-mean scale, one q=1 structured `mu` intercept may use `phylo()`, `phylo_interaction()`, `spatial()`, `animal()`, or `relmat()`, and one unlabelled intercept-plus-one-slope term may use `phylo()`, `spatial()`, `animal()`, or `relmat()`. Ordinary Poisson additionally admits unique labelled scalar ordinary intercepts on the complete-data route, including `sd(id, block = "p") ~ w`; it also admits one complete-data unlabelled `(1 + x | id)` ML-Laplace route at `point_fit_recovery` (`mc-0718`) and the exact labelled q2 intercept--slope forms for `phylo()`, `spatial()`, `animal()`, and `relmat()`; ordinary NB2 admits the matching unlabelled ordinary `(1 + x | id)` route at `point_fit_recovery` (`mc-0719`), now with separate predictor-dependent SD formulas for its intercept and slope on the complete-data ordinary route, plus one q1 phylogenetic location intercept with its matching direct-SD formula; ordinary lognormal admits the matching unlabelled ordinary `(1 + x | id)` route at `point_fit_recovery` (`mc-0720`) with coefficient-specific predictor-dependent SD formulas for its intercept and slope, and the exact phylo labelled q2 form only. These ordinary non-Gaussian q2 cells are point-fit-only and not profile-ready. Row-specific zero-inflated gates also accept an intercept-only spatial `mu` field for fixed-`zi` Poisson and fixed-`zi` NB2 at diagnostic-only grade. Ordinary Student-t, zero-truncated NB2, lognormal, Gamma, beta, beta-binomial, and binomial models also support unlabelled `mu` random intercepts and independent numeric slopes such as `(1 | id) + (0 + x | id)`. Binomial additionally admits one experimental complete-data unlabelled `(1 + x | id)` point-fit route. Beta additionally admits exactly one unlabelled intercept-only `phylo(1 | species, tree = tree)` term in `mu` at `point_fit_recovery` for the exact tested `g = 1024, m = 4` regime. The separate q1 direct-SD exception `sd(species, level = "phylogenetic") ~ w` has exact-regime profile-coverage evidence with the caveats recorded for `mc-0017`. Family `sigma` random effects are limited to listed routes; Gamma additionally supports one complete-data ordinary `sigma` intercept with `sd(group, dpar = "sigma") ~ predictors` and fixed-effect `mu`. This models variation in the latent log-CV effect; native recovery and constant-SD equivalence pass, while interval coverage and Julia parity remain unverified. Other family `sigma` combinations remain fixed-effect-only or separately scoped; phylogenetic random-effect slopes, labels/q2+, broader designs and broader interval/coverage claims remain deferred. Correlated non-Gaussian slopes outside the exact binomial, ordinary Poisson, ordinary NB2, and ordinary lognormal q2 point-fit routes (coefficient-specific predictor-dependent SDs are checked for the ordinary NB2 and lognormal q2 routes), covariance labels outside the exact count-q2 forms, pure or multiple structured count slopes, zero-inflated structured count effects outside exact local-fit gates, bounded-response exact-boundary random effects, and richer or labelled NB2 structured `sigma` effects beyond the exact q1 recovery-grade routes remain planned. For bivariate Gaussian models, matching labelled random intercepts in `mu1` and `mu2`, such as `(1 | p | id)` in both formulas, matching slope-only `mu1`/`mu2` blocks such as `(0 + x | p | id)`, matching same-response slope-only `mu1`/`sigma1` or `mu2`/`sigma2` blocks, matching one-slope q=4 location blocks such as `(1 + x | p | id)`, matching q=6 location blocks such as `(1 + x + z | p | id)`, and the first all-four q8 endpoint block with matching `(1 + x | p | id)` terms in `mu1`, `mu2`, `sigma1`, and `sigma2` are implemented. Bivariate residual-scale slope-only `sigma1`/`sigma2` blocks are listed under `sigma`; formal simulation recovery for q > 2 bivariate location and q8 blocks is later. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:1199:| `sd(group)` | Scoped direct-SD routes are implemented for one or more distinct unlabelled univariate Gaussian `mu` intercepts; selected native complete-data ordinary `mu` intercepts in Bernoulli/binomial, Poisson, NB2, Gamma, Tweedie, Beta, beta-binomial, lognormal, Student-t, and skew-normal; and one Poisson slope-only target using `sd(id, coef = "x") ~ w` with `(0 + x | id)`. Bernoulli, Poisson, Gamma, ordinary NB2, Tweedie, Beta, beta-binomial, and cumulative-logit have native-TMB ordinary-intercept and independent-slope routes. Ordinary complete-data NB2 also admits separate coefficient-selected SD formulas for one unlabelled correlated `(1 + x | id)` block, with synthetic SD-slope recovery, predictions, and constant-SD equivalence. Gamma and Beta additionally have one native q1 animal intercept SD route with pedigree, A, or Ainv input; Gamma also has a separate q1 animal slope-only coefficient route with group-level SD predictors and one q2 animal intercept-plus-independent-slope route with a separate `coef`-selected SD formula for each field. The combined Gamma q2 recovery/workflow fixture currently uses pedigree input; the separate q1 Gamma slope-only A/Ainv provider rows remain unverified. Complete-data NB2 has a separate q1 animal route for one intercept or one slope-only coefficient with group-level SD predictors; it does not admit a multi-coefficient structured block. Beta-binomial has the same three animal matrix inputs for its q1 `mu` intercept route. Beta's focused tests check point recovery, prediction, constant-SD equivalence, and supplied-matrix likelihood agreement with the pedigree fit. Beta-binomial's A/Ainv tests check point recovery, group predictions, and likelihood agreement with pedigree under a joint matrix-label permutation. Julia parity and interval calibration remain open. Julia Bernoulli/binomial SD regression, including separate coefficient-selected SD formulas and one correlated q2 block, exists only in the DRM.jl development candidate and was tested with explicitly matched local drmTMB and DRM.jl source checkouts; current shared/released DRM.jl does not include it. Candidate bridge checks cover q2 fitting, recovery, extraction, group-SD prediction, save/reload, profile routing, and three bootstrap refits. A fit emitted a near-singular-Hessian warning; this is not same-data q2 parity or interval calibration. Other Julia SD routes noted here are likewise candidate-only and require matching candidate source. The candidate Julia Poisson slope route has an independent quadrature check and one same-data engine comparison; the candidate Gamma animal route has one same-data non-unit-diagonal `A` comparison of fitted SD coefficients, log likelihood, and named group SDs. These are narrow recovery checks, not calibrated uncertainty evidence. Predictors must be constant within group after missing-row filtering. The Beta q1 phylogenetic exception `sd(spp_id, level = "phylogenetic") ~ x_tau` targets latent `tau`, not family `sigma`; ordinary and phylogenetic Beta direct-SD formulae cannot yet be combined. Explicit selectors resolve target identity; multiple coefficient-specific SD formulas on one group are implemented for separate independent Bernoulli terms, one ordinary correlated Bernoulli/binomial q = 2 block, native ordinary Poisson and NB2 q = 2 blocks, and the native Gamma animal q2 route just described. Correlated-block SD regression for other families or providers, and interval calibration, remain open. Gamma ordinary intercept and slope, Gamma animal slope-only, and Tweedie intercept and slope each have one synthetic recovery fit; Gamma intercept and ordinary slope and Tweedie intercept and slope also have constant-SD equivalence checks. Beta has one higher-information recovery fit and constant-SD equivalence check; beta-binomial has an ordinary-intercept recovery/oracle fixture and a separate independent-slope recovery, prediction, simulation, and save/reload smoke check; Student-t and skew-normal each have one recovery fit. Interval calibration, wider random-effect shapes, and remaining family routes are open. Gamma also admits one complete-data ordinary `sigma` random intercept with `sd(group, dpar = "sigma") ~ predictors` and fixed-effect `mu`; this targets the SD of the latent log-CV effect. Native recovery, conditional output, save/reload, within-group validation, and constant-SD equivalence checks pass; interval coverage and Julia parity remain open. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:1210:| `animal(1 | id, pedigree = ped)` | Implemented first slice for univariate Gaussian `mu`, univariate Gaussian `sigma`, and matching `mu`/`sigma` animal-model intercepts using a dense additive relationship matrix built from `id`, `dam`, and `sire` columns. Native Gamma q1 `mu` intercepts can use `sd(id, level = "animal") ~ z` with pedigree, A, or Ainv input, and Gamma also has a separate complete-data q1 slope-only coefficient route with a group-level SD predictor. Gamma additionally supports one complete-data q2 intercept-plus-independent-numeric-slope route with separate `sd(id, level = "animal", dpar = "mu", coef = "(Intercept)") ~ z0` and `sd(id, level = "animal", dpar = "mu", coef = "x") ~ z1` formulas; its current q2 recovery/workflow evidence uses pedigree input and does not qualify the separate q1 slope-only A/Ainv provider rows. Native Beta admits one q1 animal `mu` intercept with this SD formula through pedigree, A, or Ainv input; synthetic tests check point recovery, group-level prediction, and constant-SD equivalence. These non-Gaussian animal SD routes have no interval-calibration or Julia-parity evidence; the Gamma q1 slope-only route does not admit a multi-coefficient block. |
docs/design/01-formula-grammar.md:1214:| `animal(1 + x | id, pedigree = ped)` | Implemented one numeric animal-model random slope for univariate Gaussian `mu`; it estimates independent `animal(1 | id)` and `animal(0 + x | id)` fields with no slope correlation. The source-tested residual-scale and matched `mu+sigma` one-slope cells use `animal(1 + x | id, A = A)` as native point-fit/extractor evidence. Native Gamma also admits this q2 term with separate coefficient-selected SD formulas for the intercept and slope, using pedigree input in the current recovery/workflow fixture; zero slope loadings and the exact constant-loading/constant-SD-design confounding case are refused. This is not a correlated intercept-slope block and establishes neither interval calibration nor Julia parity. Labels such as `animal(1 + x | p | id, pedigree = ped)` are rejected until larger structured slope covariance blocks are designed. |
